    {
        "title": "From Vibe Coding to Agentic Engineering",
        "venue": "SkillUp AI | The Garage, Microsoft (India)",
        "date": "July 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-07-30",
        "category": "microsoft",
        "type": "🔬 Workshop",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/skillup-ai-from-vibe-coding-to-agentic-engineering.png",
        "description": "<p>Delivered virtually as part of SkillUp AI (The Garage, Microsoft India) to 60+ participants ahead of the Global Hackathon 2026, this workshop argued that the gap between a demo that wows on Friday and a build that survives Monday is a pipeline, not a better prompt. Rather than a lecture, the session was a live walk through six agent skills in a real repo — from interrogating a rough brief to an overnight loop that ships tested slices — backed by a companion repo attendees could clone at hour zero.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Vibe coding has a ceiling — greenfield demos lie to you, and everything you ship after the demo is brownfield: hidden callers, hidden tests, and decisions that live in people's heads instead of the repo</li><li>Every agent run starts from zero — it's a brilliant new starter fired at the end of every shift, so compacting isn't memory and files are; whatever it must know has to be on disk before the run begins</li><li>A 1M-token window buys roughly 100k tokens of real <em>thinking</em> — past that, reasoning degrades but confidence doesn't, which is why tasks must be sized to fit inside the smart zone</li><li>The move that survives both facts: stop typing code and start commissioning it — your ceiling shifts from typing speed to the quality of your decisions</li><li>Copilot already ships eight levers most people never touch — but only an Agent Skill encodes a whole <em>procedure</em>, so the agent walks the same path every time instead of improvising</li><li>Grilling inverts plan mode: instead of the agent proposing a plan for you to rubber-stamp, it interrogates you until the design holds up — every answer prunes a branch, and ADRs record the <em>why</em> so nobody \"fixes\" it back</li><li>Fire tracer bullets, not layers — cut the spec into thin vertical slices with declared blocking edges, so the frontier can be drained in parallel and each ticket labelled afk or human-in-the-loop</li><li>Ralph is genuinely just a while loop — fresh context, next unblocked ticket, TDD at agreed seams, a two-axis review of standards and spec, a decision-rich commit, repeat until there are no tasks left</li><li>Agent-written tests lie in three predictable ways — implementation-coupled, tautological, or horizontally sliced — and a passing test is the only thing the loop can actually trust</li><li>The closing thesis: stop vibing, start engineering — AI invented none of these ideas, it just made them non-optional</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260730_SkillUpAI_FromVibeCodingToAgenticEngineering.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "GitHub Repo",
                "icon": "fab fa-github",
                "url": "https://github.com/tezansahu/microsoft-idc-agentic-engineering-workshop-2026"
            },
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:share:7489352164729913345/"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Stop Vibe-Shipping Your Skills",
        "venue": "Common Ground 2026",
        "date": "July 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-07-25",
        "category": "community",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/commonground2026.jpeg",
        "description": "<p>Delivered at Common Ground 2026, this talk sharpened the \"can you prove your skill works?\" thesis into a practitioner's playbook — opening with a cold-open story of a skill that quietly never fired for half its team, and building out to a repeatable, runtime evaluation loop.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Skill Hell: abundance without evaluation — ~4k skills on one marketplace, 36.8% flawed on inspection, and ~1 in 5 curated skills actually make things worse</li><li>Evaluating a skill is really three questions: is it sound (validation), does it fire (triggering), and does it help (behaviour) — and only the first is commonly checked</li><li>Is it sound: cheap, static checks — structural validity and safety scanning — necessary, but say nothing about whether a skill fires right or helps</li><li>Does it fire: the model decides when to load a skill and gets it wrong in both directions — misfiring on the wrong prompt or staying silent on the right one</li><li>Does it help: run every case twice, with the skill and without — the delta, not a single passing run, is the whole verdict</li><li>Grade the outcome, not the path — agents reach good answers by routes you didn't script, so assertions should check results, not tool-call sequences</li><li>Two open-source skills already run this entire loop end-to-end — skill-creator and evaluate-skill-with-copilot-sdk — from generating eval sets to a reviewable scorecard</li><li>The frontier: skills that evolve themselves through a measure-propose-remeasure-keep loop, with self-discovery and self-refinement research already underway</li><li>The closing move: stop vibe-shipping skills, run the runtime checks, and let a harness prove it before you ship</li></ul>",
        "upcoming": false,
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260725_CommonGround_StopVibeShippingYourSkills.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "GitHub Repo",
                "icon": "fab fa-github",
                "url": "https://github.com/tezansahu/prove-your-agent-skill-works"
            },
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:ugcPost:7486996797077446656/"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Can You Prove Your Agent Skill Works?",
        "venue": "Azure Core Elevate, Microsoft (US & APAC Regions)",
        "date": "July 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-07-23",
        "category": "microsoft",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/azurecoreelevate-canyouproveyourskillworks.png",
        "description": "<p>Delivered as two sessions of Azure Core Elevate spread across two days for different geographies (US &amp; APAC regions), reaching close to 250 and 150 participants respectively, this talk made the case that the AI industry has automated skill creation but never automated skill judgment. Drawing on data showing that over a third of published agent skills are structurally flawed, the session argued that passing lint and looking well-authored says nothing about whether a skill actually makes an agent better at its task — and walked through a practical, repeatable recipe for proving that it does.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Skills are being mass-produced (~4k on one public marketplace) — yet 36.8% are flawed on inspection and 13.4% carry critical issues</li><li>\"Is this skill good?\" is really three questions: is it well-formed, is it safe, and does it actually work — only the third ever gets checked</li><li>Validation ≠ Evaluation: passing lint and reading well says nothing about whether a skill makes the agent better at the task</li><li>The real test: grade the outcome, not the path, and measure the delta between running with the skill and without it</li><li>SkillsBench 2026 data backs this up — curated skills lift performance +16pp on average, but ~1 in 5 tasks actually get worse</li><li>A repeatable recipe: realistic test cases, atomic assertions, and the cheapest grading method that works — from deterministic checks to LLM-as-judge</li><li>Introduced evaluate-skill-with-copilot-sdk, an open-source harness that automates with/without benchmarking and reports the delta</li><li>What's next: automatic metrics, automatic evaluation, and automatic evolution — skills that grade and improve themselves from eval signal</li><li>The closing argument: stop vibe-testing skills — measure the delta, and let a harness run the loop for you</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260722_AzureCoreElevate_CanYouProveYourAgentSkillWorks.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "GitHub Repo",
                "icon": "fab fa-github",
                "url": "https://github.com/tezansahu/prove-your-agent-skill-works"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "AI Builds the UI, Who Judges It? Agent Skills for Visual UX Evaluation of Agentic AI Systems",
        "venue": "SERI 2026 | IIIT Hyderabad",
        "date": "July 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-07-18",
        "category": "conferences",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/seri-2026.jpeg",
        "description": "<p>Delivered at the 8th edition of the Software Engineering Research in India (SERI) Conference, at SERI's first-ever Industry Day, this talk tackled a testing gap unique to agentic AI: when an MCP App renders a live widget inside an agent's reply, nothing in today's toolchain can verify that the rendered UI is actually correct. The session introduced Agent Skills as the mechanism for turning a general-purpose coding assistant into a visual UX testing orchestrator — encoding a human evaluator's Observe → Validate → Reason loop as an installable skill.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Agentic UIs compose Layout × Content × Client at runtime — and that composed experience is exactly what nothing today evaluates</li><li>Existing tools are blind to the screen: eval frameworks judge text, visual-regression needs a fixed baseline, and observability never sees the render</li><li>The fix: package a human evaluator's Observe → Validate → Reason loop into an Agent Skill — no new infrastructure required</li><li>Observe: parallel Playwright MCP workers capture the visual state by describing interactions in plain language, not brittle selectors</li><li>Validate: atomic yes/no VLM checks plus targeted assertions catch what vague, sycophancy-prone judgments miss, even across multi-turn visual state</li><li>Reason: deterministic scripts aggregate the metrics while the agent attributes each defect to the platform or a third-party partner</li><li>Two honest trade-offs — judgment variance and path non-determinism — are tamed by pushing determinism wherever possible</li><li>Already powering visual UX evals for MCP App widgets in Microsoft 365 Copilot, with richer interaction-aware checks underway</li><li>The approach generalizes to any workflow needing tool orchestration and adaptive visual judgment — accessibility, localization, consistency</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260718_SERI2026_AIBuildsTheUI-WhoJudgesIt-AgentSkillsForVisualUXEvaluationOfAgenticAI.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7484656474003189760/"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Beyond ChatGPT: Real-World AI, Innovation & Careers",
        "venue": "Bower School | T-Hub, Hyderabad ",
        "date": "June 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-06-13",
        "category": "universities",
        "type": "🔬 Workshop",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/beyond-chatgpt-bowerx.png",
        "description": "<p>Delivered at Bower School (T-Hub, Hyderabad) as two sessions for different age groups — ages 8–12 and ages 13–16 — this workshop made the same central argument from two different entry points: AI is not a black box you passively consume, it’s a tool you can understand, direct, and build with. Both sessions were structured around live demos and hands-on design exercises, with the younger group inventing their own AI helper concepts and the older group working through an agent design framework.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>AI learns by spotting patterns in examples — not by understanding them; show it enough and it finds the structure, show it biased or sparse data and it inherits the flaw (made tangible with a live Teachable Machine demo that broke the moment hand orientation changed)</li><li>Almost everyone <em>uses</em> AI — the gap, and the opportunity, is in the smaller group that builds with it: designers of behavior, not consumers of output</li><li>An LLM is a next-word predictor, not a fact retriever — it pattern-completes from training data, which is why it can be confidently wrong and why knowing the mechanics matters</li><li>Model vs. Product: the model is the engine (GPT, Claude, Gemini), the product is the car (ChatGPT, Claude.ai, Gemini app) — builders work at both layers, and the product layer is where you can create right now</li><li>Agentic AI is the real frontier: a chatbot answers and stops; an agent reasons, acts, observes, and loops until the job is done — and it’s something you can already design</li><li>The Agentic Design Canvas gives any idea an 8-box structure (Problem, User, Goal, Context, Actions, Approvals, Risks, Recovery) to turn an AI concept into something buildable and responsible</li><li>The closing line: don’t compete with AI — learn to direct it</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides (Session A)",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260613_BowerX_BeyondChatGPT_SessionA.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "Slides (Session B)",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260613_BowerX_BeyondChatGPT_SessionB.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7471788714936016897/"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Inside Build 2026",
        "venue": "SkillUp AI | The Garage, Microsoft (India + EMEA)",
        "date": "June 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-06-11",
        "category": "microsoft",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/inside-build-2026.png",
        "description": "<p>Delivered at SkillUp AI (The Garage, Microsoft — India + EMEA), this talk distilled 28 announcements from Microsoft Build 2026 — the company's biggest developer event of the year — into five coherent waves and three actionable takeaways for builders. The central thesis: Build 2026 marked a decisive shift from AI you <em>prompt</em> to AI that <em>works for you</em>, and every announcement was a piece of that architecture.</p><p><strong>The 5 Waves:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Wave 1 — Agents that Act:</strong> Scout &amp; Autopilots (always-on, identity-bearing agents that prep meetings and clear inboxes unprompted), the GitHub Copilot App (a desktop cockpit for running many coding agents in parallel), and Agent 365 (a unified control plane where every agent gets an Entra identity)</li><li><strong>Wave 2 — Microsoft's Own Brain:</strong> The MAI model family (7 in-house models covering reasoning, code, voice, and image — MAI-Thinking-1 benchmarking at Claude Opus level on coding), the Copilot Super App teased for summer, and Frontier Tuning (RL hill-climbing on your own data at ~10× lower cost than the frontier)</li><li><strong>Wave 3 — AI Leaves the Cloud:</strong> Surface RTX Spark (120B-parameter models running locally with ~1M-token context, no cloud required), Aion on Windows (an open-weight reasoner, local and unmetered), and Project Solara (an agent-first platform with a wearable badge and desk clock)</li><li><strong>Wave 4 — The Builder's Stack:</strong> Microsoft IQ (context layer grounding agents in Work + Fabric + Web data with permissions and labels respected), Azure AI Foundry Agent Framework 1.0 (11,000-model catalogue, hosted agents, self-optimizer), and Claude Opus 4.8 + GPT-5.5 as first-class Azure citizens</li><li><strong>Wave 5 — AI Does Science:</strong> Microsoft Discovery (an agentic R&amp;D platform that invented a new datacenter coolant in ~200 hours — not analyzed, <em>invented</em>) and Majorana 2 (a topological quantum chip with qubits 1,000× more reliable than the prior year, pulling practical quantum forward to 2029)</li></ul><p><strong>Key Takeaways for Builders:</strong></p><ul><li>Think in agents, not features — ask what you'd hand to a junior colleague; that task is your first agent</li><li>Build for \"yours\" — the moat isn't the model, it's grounding in your context, identity, and governance</li><li>Local is a real option now — privacy- or cost-sensitive AI no longer has to live in the cloud, and it's worth planning for on-device</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260611_SkillUpAI_InsideBuild2026.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7471055805149261824/"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Advanced Prompting & Building Your First AI Assistant",
        "venue": "AI Launchpad | Future World Alliance (Virtual)",
        "date": "June 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-06-01",
        "category": "community",
        "type": "🔬 Workshop",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/ai-launchpad-week5.jpg",
        "description": "<p>Delivered as Week 5 of AI Launchpad (Future World Alliance, virtual), this workshop made the case that writing better individual prompts is only half the skill — the other half is building AI assistants that don't need to be prompted the same way every time. The session drew a clear line between one-off prompting and <em>context engineering</em>: designing the full information environment — role, constraints, knowledge, examples, format — so your AI works right without hand-holding. Participants left with a live, working AI career advisor built from scratch in Gemini Gems (or Custom GPT).</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>The evolution from one-off prompts to persistent, reusable AI assistants — the jump is from typing to <em>designing</em></li><li>Context engineering, not just prompt engineering: you're not writing a single instruction — you're architecting the full information environment the model reasons within</li><li>CO-STAR framework — a complete system prompt checklist (Context, Objective, Style, Tone, Audience, Response Format) — so nothing critical gets missed when building an assistant</li><li>Few-Shot prompting: showing beats telling — 2–3 concrete examples transform generic output into output that mirrors exactly the pattern you need</li><li>Chain-of-Thought: asking the model to reason step-by-step before answering — surfaces leverage moves and tradeoffs that a single-shot answer reliably misses</li><li>Tree of Thoughts: exploring multiple reasoning paths simultaneously — the same mechanism behind how reasoning models like Gemini's thinking mode and o1 work internally</li><li>Structured Output: specifying the exact response format so output is immediately usable, not just readable</li><li>Prompt Chaining: breaking complex tasks into sequential steps where each output feeds the next — forces analysis before writing, so the model discovers the strategic angle instead of defaulting to the obvious one</li><li>The 3 pillars of a great AI assistant — Instructions, Knowledge, and Actions — and why the first two alone get you 80% of the way there</li><li>NotebookLM as the Knowledge pillar: visual concept maps, AI-generated podcast debates from conflicting sources, auto-slide decks, and flashcards from your own documents — the research layer that makes an assistant data-backed instead of generic</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "https://www.tezansahu.com/ai-launchpad-week-5/workshop.html"
            },
            {
                "label": "GitHub Repo",
                "icon": "fab fa-github",
                "url": "https://github.com/tezansahu/ai-launchpad-week-5"
            },
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7467448829810798593/"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "From Vibe-Coding to Agentic Engineering",
        "venue": "For 2026 Summer Interns | The Garage (India) & The University Recruiting Team, Microsoft",
        "date": "May 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-05-27",
        "category": "microsoft",
        "type": "🔬 Workshop",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/from-vibe-coding-to-agentic-engineering.jpeg",
        "description": "<p>Delivered to ~200 of Microsoft's 2026 summer interns in Hyderabad (hosted by The Garage India & the University Recruiting team), this 90-minute hands-on workshop made the case that the leap from vibe coding to <em>agentic engineering</em> is what separates a slick demo from a shippable system. Instead of a lecture, the session was structured as a live build — the presenter shipped one real feature in a real repo end-to-end, then handed the keyboards over so every intern could run the same flow on a brief of their own.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Vibe coding has a ceiling — it works on toy demos and falls apart the moment a codebase has memory, constraints, or a second contributor</li><li>The mindset flip that unlocks everything: stop being the engineer who <em>types</em>, become the architect who <em>decides</em> and the PM who <em>clarifies</em> — treat the agent as a brilliant new starter, not a senior developer</li><li>Design before code: let the agent <em>grill you</em> instead of plan for you, so the design concept comes from your judgment and the questions are grounded in the actual repo</li><li>Externalize your thinking into durable artifacts — briefs, PRDs and issues — so every downstream agent run is bound to a contract you've reviewed</li><li>Package expertise as Agent Skills so workflows become installable and reusable, not copy-pasted prompts that live in someone's head</li><li>Split the work into two loops: <em>you</em> run the outer loop (taste, design, decomposition, ship); the <em>agent</em> runs the inner loop (write, test, fix) AFK — and humans stay in the loop at the contract moments, not on every line</li><li>Build the codebase the agent has to live in: deep modules, clear contracts, and tracer-bullet slices that ship end-to-end before they scale</li><li>The closing thesis: AI is a new paradigm, but software engineering fundamentals — Brooks, Hunt &amp; Thomas, Ousterhout — work better than ever with AI, and are now <strong>non-optional</strong>. Stop vibing. Start engineering.</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260527_Garage+UniversityRecruiting_FromVibeCodingToAgenticEngineering.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "GitHub Repo",
                "icon": "fab fa-github",
                "url": "https://github.com/tezansahu/microsoft-idc-interns-agentic-engineering-workshop-2026"
            },
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/tezan-sahu_yesterday-i-watched-200-interns-break-out-activity-7465636086979989504-12Rt"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Institutionalize Your Instincts: Encoding expertise, taste, and tribal knowledge into reusable AI skills",
        "venue": "SkillUp AI | The Garage, Microsoft (India)",
        "date": "May 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-05-22",
        "category": "microsoft",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/institutionalize-your-instinct-with-agent-skills.png",
        "description": "<p>Delivered at SkillUp AI (Microsoft Garage, Hyderabad), this talk made the case that the next leap in AI productivity won't come from a smarter model — it will come from us getting better at packaging our own expertise into something the model can actually load. The session introduced Agent Skills as the open-standard mechanism for turning tribal knowledge, taste, and hard-won judgment into installable, reusable AI capabilities, and ended with a live build of a working skill on stage in under five minutes.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Every team has the person whose AI outputs are just better — the problem is that \"better\" lives in their head and disappears the moment they log off</li><li>The mindset flip: stop writing prompts, start installing skills — expertise the agent <em>holds</em>, not expertise you <em>carry</em></li><li>A Skill is almost embarrassingly simple: a folder with a SKILL.md (name, description, body) plus optional scripts, references, and assets — no SDK, no framework, no deployment pipeline</li><li>Progressive disclosure is the trick that makes skills scale: name+description are always-on, the body loads on match, sub-files load on-demand — install 50 skills and only 1–2 ever sit in the context window</li><li>Skills are not agents: skills are packaged expertise (15–30 min to build, portable across tools); agents are autonomous loops (days/weeks, framework-bound)</li><li>It's an open standard — Anthropic, GitHub Copilot, Cursor, Codex, Windsurf and more all consume the same format. Same playbook as MCP, but for expertise instead of tools</li><li>The highest-leverage skills are the ones built for your role and your team — engineers, data folks, PMs, finance, HR, consulting — not generic ones</li><li>You can build a useful Skill in under five minutes — the bottleneck is no longer engineering, it's deciding what tribal knowledge is worth institutionalising</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260522_SkillUpAI_InstitutionalizeYourInstinctsWithAgentSkills.pdf"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Your AI Never Once Said \"You're Wrong\"",
        "venue": "Manifest 2.0",
        "date": "May 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-05-17",
        "category": "community",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/your-ai-never-once-said-youre-wrong.jpg",
        "description": "<p>Delivered at Manifest 2.0, this talk tackled one of the most under-discussed failure modes in modern AI products: <em>sycophancy</em> — the model telling you what you want to hear instead of what you need to know. The session walked through the behaviour, the mechanism that produces it, the blindspot in standard product metrics that hides it, and a concrete five-lever toolkit builders can apply to push back against it.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Sycophancy isn't a hallucination or a lie — it's the model agreeing with you, and it's the most invisible failure mode in AI products today</li><li>It surfaces everywhere builders ship: coding assistants that bend to a developer's stated confidence, multi-agent systems where dissent collapses into consensus, and chatbots that endorsed harmful behaviour in 32% of tested high-stakes scenarios</li><li>The mechanism is structural — RLHF rewards agreeable responses, which makes successive models more sycophantic, which then win A/B tests because users rate them more highly</li><li>Users rate sycophantic models as <em>unbiased</em> and honest ones as <em>highly biased</em> — your satisfaction metric is actively hiding the problem</li><li>Every other failure mode has a signal; sycophancy doesn't — the dashboard will stay green right up until it breaks something important</li><li>Five levers to fight it: objective evals (premise-flip, signal-delta, pressure-persistence), \"ask don't tell\" prompting, devil's-advocate UX, multi-agent + RAG architecture fixes, and making sycophancy evals a launch-blocking gate</li><li>Open-source tools like Anthropic's Petri and Bloom let you benchmark this today</li><li>Sycophancy is also a security vector — praise-based manipulation has been shown to bypass safety guardrails in major models</li><li>This is ultimately a product decision, not a technical one: comfortable lies cost more than uncomfortable truths</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260517_Manifest2.0_YourAINeverOnceSaidYouAreWrong.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7463817675845279744/"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "The Vibes will Run Out: \"Taste\" as the New Organizational Operating System",
        "venue": "BharatAI Conference | India Electronics Week 2026 | Bengaluru, India",
        "date": "May 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-05-15",
        "category": "conferences",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/the-vibes-will-run-out.jpg",
        "description": "<p>Delivered at the BharatAI Conference (India Electronics Week 2026, Bengaluru), this talk argued that the vibe-coding era is hitting its limits — and what becomes valuable next is the one thing AI can't generate on its own: <em>taste</em>. Drawing on research, industry data, and historical parallels from desktop publishing to aviation automation, the session reframed taste as an organizational capability and made the case for building the \"conductor layer\" that sits above the tool.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>AI has collapsed the barriers to building — but the prototype-to-product boundary is where most teams quietly fall apart</li><li>The evidence is in: AI-co-authored code shows ~1.7x more major issues and ~2.7x more security vulnerabilities; developers feel 20% faster while actually being 19% slower</li><li>Four crises are unfolding at once: the Invisible Standard, the Ownership Gap, the Expectation Asymmetry, and the Tool Proliferation Trap</li><li>Access always outpaces mastery — desktop publishing and cockpit automation both proved this; vibe coding is the next chapter of the same story</li><li>When capability commoditises, value migrates up — scaffolding and first drafts are getting cheaper; evaluation, system design, and production stewardship are appreciating</li><li>Taste is trained judgment about what will work in the messy real world — knowing when to stop, what <em>not</em> to build, and which decisions will age well</li><li>Taste can be institutionalised by mining your best work, codifying blueprints, and injecting them upstream of AI generation</li><li>The closing line: <em>the vibes will always run out — what remains is the judgment you built along the way</em></li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/20260515_BharatAI_TheVibesWillRunOut.pdf"
            },
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7462713739423780864/"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "AIoT on the Cloud: Selecting the Right Hosting Solutions",
        "venue": "IoTShow.in | India Electronics Week 2026 | Bengaluru, India",
        "date": "May 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-05-13",
        "category": "conferences",
        "type": "🎤 Panel Discussion",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/aiot-on-the-cloud.jpg",
        "description": "<p>Participated as a panelist at IoTShow.in (India Electronics Week 2026, Bengaluru) alongside leaders from Jio, ThingZo, Shalaka Connected Devices, and independent consulting. The panel tackled one of the most consequential architectural questions of the AIoT era — where intelligence should actually live, and how to pick the right hosting model for it — cutting through the cloud-vs-edge hype to surface the real tradeoffs for builders deploying AI on connected devices at scale.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>IoT tells you what happened; AIoT tells you what to do about it — intelligence now lives at every layer, not just in a cloud dashboard</li><li>Latency budget drives architecture: ultra-low-latency decisions belong on-device or at the edge; learning and analytics belong in the cloud</li><li>Hybrid wins for most real deployments — train in the cloud, infer at the edge, sync opportunistically</li><li>Most AI hosting mistakes come from treating AI like just another microservice — it's a physics problem, not a scaling problem</li><li>Edge AI has crossed a tipping point: TinyML, NVIDIA Jetson, Ollama, and SLMs like Phi/Llama-3.2 make local inference cheap and capable</li><li>Stanford's Minions Protocol shows local + cloud collaboration can deliver ~98% of GPT-4 quality at a fraction of the cost</li><li>Security must be designed in from silicon up — default passwords and unpatched firmware are still the dominant attack vectors</li><li>Data sovereignty (DPDP, GDPR) makes \"just put it in the cloud\" a non-starter for entire industries</li><li>Open protocols (MQTT, ONNX, containerised edge workloads) are the best antidote to vendor lock-in</li><li>India's AIoT opportunity is to leapfrog — skip expensive edge GPUs and deploy intelligence on $5 microcontrollers and affordable SLMs</li><li>The next frontier is the Internet of Agents — sensors as tools, actuators as actions, and a hard new evaluation problem when AI controls the physical world</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7462351344742322176/"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Hello, Agent! — Building AI Agents with Microsoft Agent Framework",
        "venue": "IntelliPaat School of Technology, Hyderabad (Guru Nanak University)",
        "date": "April 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-04",
        "category": "universities",
        "type": "🔬 Workshop",
        "image": "assets/images/speaking-engagements/ist-gnu-hello-agent.jpg",
        "description": "<p>A hands-on, zero-to-agent workshop delivered to students at IntelliPaat School of Technology (Guru Nanak University), walking participants through every layer of AI agent development using Microsoft Agent Framework. Rather than staying theoretical, the session was structured around a live lab in which students built a fully functional Campus Management Agent from scratch — wiring up tools, crafting system instructions, and managing multi-turn conversations.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>Agents vs. chatbots: the critical differences — autonomy, tool use, stateful reasoning, and proactive action</li><li>Core agent anatomy: models, tools, memory, orchestration, guardrails, and output handlers — and how they fit together</li><li>Tool design principles: descriptive naming, LLM-oriented docstrings, typed annotations with Pydantic, and graceful error handling are not optional</li><li>Auto-generating tool schemas from well-annotated Python functions: good code and good AI integration are the same thing</li><li>System prompt engineering as a first-class artifact: vague instructions break agents; specificity in tool-use sequencing and information-gathering rules is essential</li><li>Session management enables genuine multi-turn usefulness — agents that remember context across a conversation are fundamentally more helpful</li><li>Production realities: latency, cost, observability, and the dangers of tool overload, no evaluation, and over-engineering too early</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/2020604_IST-GNU_HelloAgentBuildingAIAgentsWithMicrosoftAgentFramework.pdf"
            },
            {
