    {
        "title": "AI Agents in Talent Acquisition",
        "venue": "Learning Day | Global Talent Acquisition (GTA) Asia, Microsoft",
        "date": "April 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-04",
        "category": "microsoft",
        "type": "🎙️ Podcast",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/ai-agents-in-talent-acquisition.png",
        "description": "<p>Delivered at Microsoft Global Talent Acquisition (GTA) Asia Learning Day for an audience of close to 100, this session combined talks, a podcast-style conversation, and a live demo to show how AI agents can improve recruiting workflows end-to-end - from drafting job descriptions to extracting hiring signals and accelerating sourcing - while staying grounded in quality, consistency, and responsible human oversight.</p><p><strong>Key Takeaways:</strong></p><ul><li>AI is most effective in talent acquisition when it augments recruiter workflows, not replaces recruiter judgment</li><li>High-value use cases include JD creation, skills/keyword extraction, and generating Boolean strings for sourcing</li><li>Reliable recruiting agents need clear instructions, scoped capabilities, relevant connectors, and guided conversation starters</li><li>Grounding outputs on approved templates and knowledge sources improves consistency and trust</li><li>Adoption improves when teams start with repeatable scenarios and iteratively refine prompts based on real usage</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "LinkedIn Post",
                "icon": "fab fa-linkedin",
                "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/tezan-sahu_ai-talentacquisition-microsoftcopilot-share-7454354326807822336-cmhz"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "What Does Your Agent Remember About You? (And Should It?)",
        "venue": "AI Conference 2026 | Tech Mahindra, Hyderabad",
        "date": "April 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-04",
        "category": "conferences",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/ai-conference-2026.png",
        "description": "<p>Delivered as the youngest speaker at AI Conference 2026, this talk tackled the often-overlooked challenge of memory in AI agent systems. Using a relatable analogy — a friend who remembers every embarrassing thing you’ve ever done and brings it up at the worst moment — the session explored why agents need memory, what types exist, and the real risks when memory goes wrong.</p><p><strong>Key Topics Covered:</strong></p><ul><li>The Amnesia Problem — why stateless sessions force users to re-explain everything, every time</li><li>A taxonomy of four memory types and how each works in practice</li><li>Real-world implementations: who’s using agent memory and what they store</li><li>Three failure modes: staleness (outdated facts), poisoning (injected false info), and privacy leaks</li><li>Ethics, regulation, and governance — should agents remember everything?</li><li>Open problems the field hasn’t solved yet</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/202604_AgentConference2026_WhatDoesYourAgentRememberAboutYou.pdf"
