    {
        "title": "Agent Skills: Turning Tribal Knowledge into Plug-and-play Leverage",
        "venue": "KITE (Design) & Copilot Extensibility Teams, Microsoft",
        "date": "March 2026",
        "sortDate": "2026-03",
        "category": "microsoft",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/agent-skills.png",
        "description": "<p>Presented to Microsoft's KITE (Design) and Copilot Extensibility teams, this session introduced Agent Skills — a new paradigm for packaging tribal knowledge into reusable, plug-and-play modules for AI coding agents. The talk made the case that prompt libraries fail because of discovery friction and personalization gaps, and showed how skills solve this by auto-loading contextual expertise only when needed.</p><p><strong>Key Topics Covered:</strong></p><ul><li>The problem: repeated context copy-pasting, knowledge trapped in heads, and prompt libraries nobody uses</li><li>What Agent Skills are: folder-based packages (SKILL.md + scripts + references) — no SDK, no framework, no deployment pipeline</li><li>Progressive disclosure: how skills scale where system prompts don't (install 50, only 1–2 load per task)</li><li>Skills vs. Agents: packaged expertise for existing agents vs. autonomous loops with frameworks</li><li>The open standard ecosystem: write once, use in Claude Code, GitHub Copilot, Codex, Cursor, and 10+ tools</li><li>Skill ideas built for the room: design-review, spec-writer, a11y-audit, m365-extension-spec, and more</li><li><strong>Live demo:</strong> Building a skill in under 5 minutes using the skill-creator skill</li><li>Design principles for great skills and the sharing paradox (org-wide vs. team-level vs. personal)</li></ul>",
        "links": []
    },
    {
        "title": "Powering India's AI Tomorrow",
        "venue": "AI Student Summit 2026 | KLH University, Hyderabad",
