        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Tool Use & MCP",
        "venue": "AI Uncovered Masterclass, STCI Microsoft",
        "date": "August 2025",
        "sortDate": "2025-08",
        "category": "microsoft",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/tool-use-&-mcp.jpg",
        "description": "<p>Co-presented with Kanishk Lal as part of the AI Uncovered Masterclass at STCI Microsoft, this session provided a comprehensive walkthrough of tool use in LLMs and the Model Context Protocol (MCP). Starting from the evolution of function calling (ReAct, Toolformer, OpenAI API), the talk built up to MCP as the solution to the M×N integration problem, covering its architecture, primitives, and security considerations — all anchored by three live demos.</p><p><strong>Key Topics Covered:</strong></p><ul><li>Evolution of function/tool calling: ReAct pattern, Toolformer, and OpenAI's Function Calling API</li><li>The M×N integration problem and how MCP transforms it into M+N</li><li>MCP architecture: Hosts, Clients, Servers (local &amp; remote), and transport mechanisms (STDIO, Streamable HTTP)</li><li>MCP primitives: Tools, Resources, Resource Templates, Prompts, and Interactive UI Components</li><li><strong>Demo 1:</strong> Creating a basic local MCP server in C# and running it in VS Code</li><li><strong>Demo 2:</strong> Leveraging existing MCP servers (Playwright + MS SQL) for product research on Amazon</li><li><strong>Demo 3:</strong> Transforming a fine-tuned DistilBERT sentiment classifier API into a conversational assistant via MCP</li><li>Security threats in MCP (Tool Poisoning, Puppet/Tool Shadowing, Rug Pull attacks) and mitigations</li><li>UTCP (Universal Tool Calling Protocol) as an emerging alternative and its tradeoffs vs. MCP</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/202508_AIUncoveredMasterclass_ToolUse&MCP.pdf"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "title": "Multi-Agent Systems with LLMs for NLP",
        "venue": "AI Uncovered Masterclass, STCI Microsoft",
        "date": "August 2025",
        "sortDate": "2025-08",
        "category": "microsoft",
        "type": "🗣️ Talk",
        "image": "./assets/images/speaking-engagements/muli-agent-systems-with-llms-for-nlp.jpg",
        "description": "<p>Co-presented with Gurparkash Singh and Shubhojit Mallick as part of the AI Uncovered Masterclass at STCI Microsoft, this session covered the full spectrum of LLM-based AI agents and multi-agent systems. The talk progressed from foundational agent concepts and agentic design patterns through to multi-agent architectures, communication paradigms, and observability — punctuated by live demos building real multi-agent workflows with Microsoft's AutoGen framework.</p><p><strong>Key Topics Covered:</strong></p><ul><li>LLM-based AI agents: what they are, how they differ from traditional rule-based agents, and the paradigm shift with LLMs as reasoning engines</li><li>Automation vs. Workflows vs. AI Agents — clarifying the distinctions</li><li>Memory in AI agents: debunking misconceptions (context window ≠ memory, RAG ≠ memory) and types of agent memory</li><li>The AI agent stack and agentic design patterns</li><li>Multi-Agent Systems (MAS): why they matter (complexity, scalability, robustness, specialization) and common agent roles</li><li>Agent communication: cooperative, competitive, and debate paradigms; centralized vs. decentralized structures; message passing vs. shared memory</li><li><strong>Demo:</strong> Blog-to-Twitter Thread Scheduler using a sequential multi-agent pipeline</li><li><strong>Demo:</strong> Marketing Campaign Creator using a swarm-based multi-agent architecture</li><li>Observability in MAS: transparency, interpretability, key metrics, and frameworks</li><li>Designing resilient AI agents: handling loops, timeouts, network failures, corrupted inputs, and harmful content</li><li>Computer-Using Agents (CUA): Claude Computer Use, OpenAI Operator, Browser-Use, Manus AI, and UI-TARS</li></ul>",
        "links": [
            {
                "label": "Slides",
                "icon": "fas fa-file-pdf",
                "url": "./assets/data/resources/202508_AIUncoveredMasterclass_LLMBasedAIAgents&MultiAgentSystems.pdf"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
